WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.276
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.133 --> 00:00:07.550
DINO CHIESA: We've published
APIs in the developer portal.

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:12.290
Now let's look at
monitoring and analytics.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:16.610
Apigee Edge includes a number
of built-in analytics charts.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:20.360
The most basic is the
traffic volume chart.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.580
Here you can see traffic
volumes over time--

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.230
over the week,
the day, the hour.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:28.220
You can also select
custom time periods.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.340
All of this is powered by
Google Cloud Analytics,

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.920
so you get great performance
from these charts even

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.340
with huge API traffic volumes.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.820
That's traffic volume,
but thinking outside-in,

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.250
we'd like to understand
the experience users are

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:45.810
getting from the APIs.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:49.020
What's the response time?

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.880
Here we can see the median
response time as well as

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.830
the 95th percentile and
the 99th percentile.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.480
Analyzing these
trends can help you

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.880
identify when your
backend services start

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.670
to show strain under load.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.530
In fact, Apigee
Edge analytics can

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.390
be a major benefit
of Apigee Edge

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:12.210
even if you don't perform
transforms or apply security

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.890
in your proxy.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.530
This response time
insight can be invaluable

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:22.600
as you perform A/B tests
or roll out new services.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.580
Now let's look at
error reporting.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.520
In any high-volume system,
there will be errors.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.100
Some are good errors,
like a 401 when

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.470
an expired token is presented.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.600
Some are unexpected.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.340
With the error code
analysis chart,

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:37.960
you can examine
error code trends

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.790
and better understand where the
errors are occurring and why.

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:44.485
This chart can give
you that clarity.

00:01:47.202 --> 00:01:49.410
These are just a few of the
analytics charts that are

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.710
available out-of-the-box
with Apigee Edge,

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:56.130
and you also have the ability to
create custom reports that show

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.780
data you care about.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.050
Which clients are trending up?

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:04.380
Which device types
show the most errors?

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.060
How does traffic
volume vary over

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.970
the course of a day or a week?

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.979
How effective is the cache?

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:13.893
Which versions of my
API are in low use

00:02:13.893 --> 00:02:15.060
that I might deprecate them?

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:21.030
Using Apigee Edge analytics gets
you visibility into your API

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.220
program that you
absolutely need in order

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:26.400
to understand usage and
manage your API efforts.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

